  "primary_goal": "Distinguish shared themes, partial textual reception, later reinterpretation, political conflict and distinct institutional paths before and during the sixteenth-century Reformation.",
  "historiographic_notes": [
    "Do not treat Wyclif, Hus and Luther as one direct student-teacher chain or identical Eucharistic theology.",
    "Four Articles 1420/1421 formation, Basel 1433 negotiation and 1436 Jihlava promulgation are distinct stages.",
    "Do not claim all vernacular Bible manuscript ownership was Lollard or banned without exception.",
    "Valdes 1170s origin and Waldensian 1532 reform connections differ; 'Chanforan' terminology is later.",
    "Modern Devotion and reform within established late medieval church traditions are independent important histories, not failed Protestantism.",
    "Post-1415 Hussite movement is not coterminous with lifetime positions of Hus.",
    "Women and lay devotional communities must not disappear from a male-only reformer genealogy.",
    "Document, political and social relationships must not be visualized as unjustified teacher-to-student causal arrows."
  ],
